# Example requests for the Three-Kid Family service, to try it by hand (IntelliJ HTTP Client, or the VS Code REST
# Client extension).
#
# Start the service fresh (people are kept in memory, a restart begins empty):
#   make run                                   (Docker, builds the image first)
#   ./mvnw -pl service spring-boot:run         (without Docker)
# Then run the requests from top to bottom: each answer depends on the requests before it.
#
# IDs: Ada 42 (the example from the casus), Bart 101, Lisa 102, Maggie 103, Marge 104, Homer 105.
# Only Maggie (born 2020) is under 18. "Today" is the date in Europe/Amsterdam (ADR 0007).

@host = http://localhost:8080

### 0 — Expect 200 with the version of the build that runs
GET {{host}}/api/version

### 1 — Expect 444, no body: Ada (the casus example) has a partner but only two children, and nobody else matches
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id"       :  42,
  "name"     : "Ada Lovelace",
  "birthDate": "1815-12-10",
  "parent1"  : { "id": 1 },
  "parent2"  : { "id": 2 },
  "partner"  : { "id": 99 },
  "children" : [{ "id": 77 }, { "id": 78 }]
}

### 2 — Expect 444: Bart, with parents Homer and Marge (neither registered yet)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": 101, "name": "Bart Simpson", "birthDate": "1999-04-01", "parent1": { "id": 105 }, "parent2": { "id": 104 } }

### 3 — Expect 444: Lisa, with parents Homer and Marge
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": 102, "name": "Lisa Simpson", "birthDate": "2001-05-09", "parent1": { "id": 105 }, "parent2": { "id": 104 } }

### 4 — Expect 444: Maggie (under 18), with parents Homer and Marge; nobody names a partner yet
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": 103, "name": "Maggie Simpson", "birthDate": "2020-01-12", "parent1": { "id": 105 }, "parent2": { "id": 104 } }

### 5 — Expect 200 with Marge and Homer. Homer has no record yet, but matches as a person known only by ID
#       (ADR 0010): he is shown with only his ID, partner Marge (who names him) and the three children who name him.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": 104, "name": "Marge Simpson", "birthDate": "1978-03-19",
  "partner": { "id": 105 },
  "children": [{ "id": 101 }, { "id": 102 }, { "id": 103 }]
}

### 6 — Expect 200 with Homer and Marge, now Homer with his own record
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": 105, "name": "Homer Simpson", "birthDate": "1976-05-12",
  "partner": { "id": 104 },
  "children": [{ "id": 101 }, { "id": 102 }, { "id": 103 }]
}

### 7 — Expect 444: an update of Maggie makes her an adult, so no child is under 18 (a POST of a known ID replaces)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": 103, "name": "Maggie Simpson", "birthDate": "2003-01-12", "parent1": { "id": 105 }, "parent2": { "id": 104 } }

### 8 — Expect 200 with Homer and Marge: Maggie's birth date corrected back
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": 103, "name": "Maggie Simpson", "birthDate": "2020-01-12", "parent1": { "id": 105 }, "parent2": { "id": 104 } }

### 9 — Expect 204, no body: delete Maggie; her ID is removed from every record and ignored from now on
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

[103]

### 10 — Expect 204 again: deleting the same ID twice changes nothing
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

[103]

### 11 — Expect 444: Homer still names Maggie, but a deleted ID does not count, so he has two children
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": 105, "name": "Homer Simpson", "birthDate": "1976-05-12",
  "partner": { "id": 104 },
  "children": [{ "id": 101 }, { "id": 102 }, { "id": 103 }]
}

### 12 — Expect 444: Maggie is posted again, but a deleted ID is ignored for storage and matching (ADR 0014)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/people
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": 103, "name": "Maggie Simpson", "birthDate": "2020-01-12", "parent1": { "id": 105 }, "parent2": { "id": 104 } }
